<section class="im-section im-wrap">
	<div class="im-prose">
		<h2>Three cards answer "which one", a table answers "what exactly"</h2>
		<p>A site with tiers needs both. The cards are read first and fast, so every column has <strong>the same number of rows</strong> and the differences fall on the <strong>same lines</strong> — which is only possible if a tier lists what it does <em>not</em> include rather than omitting the row. A missing row is read as an oversight; a struck one is read as an answer.</p>

		<h2>The recommended tier fills, it does not grow</h2>
		<p><code>im-tier-lead</code> is a surface and a heavier frame. Scaling it up by 5% — the usual trick — breaks the alignment of every row inside it, which is the one thing the set exists to provide.</p>

		<h2>Both prices are in the markup</h2>
		<p>The monthly and yearly figures are both there and CSS shows one, keyed off <code>data-period</code> on the container. Nothing is computed, nothing is fetched, and the page is correct before any script runs — so there is no moment where the button says "yearly" and the number is still monthly.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th></th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>im-tiers[data-period]</code></td><td>The set. <code>month</code> or <code>year</code>.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-tier-lead</code> · <code>im-tier-flag</code></td><td>The recommendation, marked once.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-tier-list > li[data-no]</code></td><td>Not included — listed, struck, and kept on its line.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-compare</code></td><td>The table underneath, for the second read.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p>In a theme the columns come from <code>{{"{{#get \"tiers\"}}"}}</code> and the prices from <code>{{"{{price}}"}}</code>; Ghost gives up to three paid tiers plus free, which is exactly what this grid is sized for.</p>
	</div>
</section>
